<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
<!--
  Which rubric this deal is adjudicated under. Ruled on Matthew's word, session 16.

  Until now the binding was AAO_Rubric.active, a static set by whoever started the run. That
  is enough for one rubric per org and not enough for the case the scoping law exists to
  survive: a customer edits their rubric mid-quarter, two generations are live at once, and
  deals opened before the edit are still judged under the old one. A static cannot express
  that, because it is a property of the execution and the question is a property of the deal.

  Written once, at cold start or first adjudication, and then left alone. Not written again
  on later passes: a deal that has already been judged under a rubric keeps being judged
  under it, which is what makes its claims comparable to each other.

  A field on the standard Opportunity, which is a deliberate exception to nothing. The
  standing constraint forbids fields on managed ALTF objects and triggers on objects we do
  not own. This is neither: it is our own field on a standard object, and no trigger reads
  or writes it.
-->
    <fullName>AAO_Rubric_Version__c</fullName>
    <description>Written by: cold start, or the first adjudication on this deal, once. Read by: AAO_Rubric, to scope every contract read to the rubric this deal is judged under. Blank means nothing has adjudicated this deal yet.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>The rubric generation this deal is adjudicated under. Set once, at first adjudication, and never changed: claims on this deal were judged against this rubric.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>AAO Rubric Version</label>
    <type>Text</type>
    <length>40</length>
    <required>false</required>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
